{"canonical_record":{"canonical_payload_sha256":"3cf863947b7c2cb26aef81409b3f14c3b55809b06feb069f579d261f1d392042","full_review_record_id":"fullrev_f47f6ec02cfedfcf95636795baf93c945f15878b3b14e5c580d750626de97c99","overall_assessment":"The paper presents a technically sound framework that integrates neural network predictions with search-based program synthesis. The methodological approach is well-motivated, and the experimental results demonstrate significant speedups over baselines. However, the evaluation relies on a small test set and lacks statistical variance reporting, which limits the robustness of the empirical claims. Reproducibility is hindered by the absence of code and specific hyperparameter details.","paper_id":"ByldLrqlx","paper_identity_sha256":"20f56211a047f81911d7697d64dc41f19122b5161f693bcc593e43c3bbc0de2f","prompt_identity_sha256":"98432b03e6b53186d060b20ac1b171b18f16bf22dbacd22e1cd7ec3520461963","raw_response_sha256":"77e083b28b104d8582572637c7468684fed040c1b97fbe3b63ef27d2b2168389","request_identity_sha256":"9412afc699a8088bd4284cd5814095d2b2994b73e4f3e77c1db3c605aa0dc4d2","reviewer_identity_sha256":"32b29c26fbf1508e066dfb25b1a9a3fe0654c5869a7da3020e1e8e61940befe3","role":"QUAL","schema_version":"agentreview-full-review-corpus-v4","structural_context_sha256":"408f86026c892182c3e6e0404121c21d05e372c7ac0adadc1eecbb7e4ec8b21f","subdimension_sections":[{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-011","SRC-012","SRC-015","SRC-016","SRC-023","SRC-024"],"review_text":"The proposed LIPS framework is technically sound and well-formulated. The decomposition of the problem into a DSL, data generation, a machine learning model for attribute prediction, and a guided search procedure is logically consistent. The choice of binary attributes for high-level functions is appropriate for the defined DSL, and the theoretical justification for using marginal probabilities to guide search (via Rank loss) is provided in Section 4.5. The integration of the neural network with existing solvers like DFS and Sketch is a valid architectural choice that leverages the strengths of both components.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-MET"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-025","SRC-026","SRC-027","SRC-028","SRC-030","SRC-031"],"review_text":"The experimental design includes comparisons against multiple search baselines (DFS, Enumeration, Sketch) and a neural network baseline (Beam search). The evaluation of generalization across program lengths is a useful addition. However, the test set size is small (P=100 programs), which may not be sufficient to robustly validate the claimed order-of-magnitude speedups. The experiments are limited to a specific DSL and integer data types, and the paper does not evaluate performance on more complex data structures or larger, more diverse datasets. The reliance on a single run for the main results without variance reporting is a limitation.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-EXP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-019","SRC-020","SRC-021","SRC-022"],"review_text":"The paper provides some implementation details, such as the embedding size (E=20), number of hidden layers (H=3), and units per layer (K=256). However, critical details for reproduction are missing, including the specific hyperparameters for training (learning rate, batch size, optimizer), the exact data generation procedure (number of programs generated, pruning criteria), and the code availability. The paper does not provide a link to the code or a detailed appendix with all necessary parameters, which hinders independent verification and reproduction of the results.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-REP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-002","SRC-008","SRC-009","SRC-032","SRC-033"],"review_text":"The paper compares its approach to relevant prior work, including differentiable interpreters (Gaunt et al., 2016), SMT-based solvers (Sketch, Brahma), and machine learning for program synthesis (Menon et al., 2013). The comparison with Menon et al. (2013) is particularly relevant, highlighting the difference in scale and model capacity. The paper also discusses the limitations of differentiable interpreters and the advantages of integrating neural networks with search-based techniques. The related work section is comprehensive and positions the contribution clearly within the field.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-CMP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-025","SRC-026","SRC-028"],"review_text":"The paper reports mean times to solve a certain percentage of test tasks but does not provide confidence intervals, standard deviations, or significance tests. The small test set size (P=100) and the lack of variance reporting make it difficult to assess the statistical significance of the reported speedups. The results are presented as single-run outcomes, which is a common practice in some areas but limits the robustness of the empirical claims. The paper does not discuss the variability in performance across different test programs or the impact of random initialization on the results.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-STA"}]},"disposition":"CANONICAL_FULL_REVIEW_COMMITTED","item_commit_sha256":"5be5a78d4afbc8d5c03544f82a355d17615f0c6a904c63d5dcccdfbd3e232f29","paper_id":"ByldLrqlx","paper_ordinal":78,"raw_attempts":[{"assistant_content_base64":"","assistant_content_sha256":"77e083b28b104d8582572637c7468684fed040c1b97fbe3b63ef27d2b2168389","attempt_index":0,"error":"","finish_reason":"stop","paper_id":"ByldLrqlx","raw_provider_response_base64":"","raw_provider_response_sha256":"35cbb5c2e3784da1fe11dc2d3750d32dc911fe12cda85d99438236429080a022","request_identity_sha256":"9412afc699a8088bd4284cd5814095d2b2994b73e4f3e77c1db3c605aa0dc4d2","role":"QUAL","schema_version":"no-memory-full-review-corpus-build-v6","usage":{"completion_tokens":1099,"prompt_tokens":16380,"prompt_tokens_details":null,"total_tokens":17479},"valid":true}],"request_identity_sha256":"9412afc699a8088bd4284cd5814095d2b2994b73e4f3e77c1db3c605aa0dc4d2","role":"QUAL","role_ordinal":0,"schema_version":"corpus-build-bootstrap-checkpoint-v4"}